<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hippocrates</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 460–c. 370 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Hippocrates of Kos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hippocrates was a Greek physician from the island of Kos, born around 460 BCE, who is traditionally called the father of medicine. He taught that diseases had natural causes rather than divine ones, and a large body of medical writings, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippocratic-corpus">Hippocratic Corpus</a>, was attributed to him and his followers, though few can be assigned to him with certainty. He is said to have died at Larissa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hippocrates.jpg" alt="Hippocrates" width="320" height="470" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified engraver · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hippocrates.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Hellenistic and Roman periods, Greek was the language of science and scholarship: Euclid’s Elements of geometry, the works of Archimedes, the medical writings of <b>Hippocrates</b>’ school and of Galen, the astronomy of Ptolemy, and the critical scholarship of the Library of Alexandria, which edited and preserved the classics.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-greek">Ancient Greek · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hippocrates</b> was born there c. 460 BCE and the Coan school of medicine bears his name.</blockquote><a href="/state/kos">Kos</a></li><li><blockquote>Later Larissa welcomed the sophist Gorgias, and <b>Hippocrates</b> died there.</blockquote><a href="/state/larissa">Larissa</a></li></ul></section>
